# Python-generated files
__pycache__/
*.py[oc]
build/
dist/
wheels/
*.egg-info

# Virtual environments
.venv

# Evaluation corpus — an external input with unresolved licensing; never commit it.
# Anchored, and matching the data file too: the corpus is a single JSONL file, so a
# directory pattern alone would not stop `git add -A` committing it.
/corpus/
bionumbers*.jsonl
*.corpus.jsonl
# The conventional places a corpus gets put, ignored by name rather than by trust.
# This branch pushes to a course remote as well as a private one, and a corpus that
# lands here would publish; the protection must not depend on remembering to read
# it in place. Add a directory here before putting data in it, not after.
/data/
/datasets/
/raw/
*.jsonl.gz

# The corpus and the export it came from. The published BioNumbers data has
# unresolved licensing (ADR-0170 on feat/ask-agent), so neither the 27 MB source
# nor any corpus built from it may enter this repository. The ingester exists so
# a reader fetches the export themselves and builds the corpus locally.
data/
# Belt and braces for a corpus written outside data/, and matching the entries on
# the sibling branches so all three checkouts refuse the same filenames.
bionumbers*.jsonl
*.corpus.jsonl
*.jsonl.manifest.json
# Each of those is written through a `.partial` first. A kill that skips the
# cleanup leaves one behind, and a suffix rule above does not match it.
*.partial

# Secrets — see CLAUDE.md, "Never commit secrets"
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
# Streamlit's secrets file: the OIDC client secret, the cookie signing key and
# the database URL (which carries a password) all live here. Ignored by path
# and by bare name -- Streamlit reads `.streamlit/secrets.toml` beside the app,
# but a copy left at the repo root is the same secret on a committable path.
.streamlit/secrets.toml
secrets.toml

# Agent runtime state — regenerable, and session content should not be committed
checkpoints/
logs/
eval-results/
# The evaluation's own output. Every record carries the retrieval results the
# agent was shown, verbatim, so a results file holds corpus text -- the same
# unlicensed data as the corpus itself (ADR-0170). `evaluation.py` also asks git
# directly before writing, because an ignore rule only protects the repositories
# someone remembered to edit.
evaluations/
*.results.jsonl

# Coverage output — regenerated by `pytest --cov`
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
